    \toprule
    Track & Name & Core focus \\
    \midrule
    RT1 & Quantum Correlations  & Many-body entanglement; strongly correlated systems \\
    RT2 & Quantum Dynamics      & Time evolution; driven systems; decoherence mitigation \\
    RT3 & Quantum Readout       & Measurement; sensing; metrology \\
    RT4 & Method Development    & Algorithms, ML, open software, hardware benchmarking \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
  \end{center}
  \vspace{3pt}
  \begin{block}{Overall structure}
    All four tracks are \textbf{tightly coupled} and span three research areas:
    \emph{Atoms \& Molecules} \textbullet\
    \emph{Nanoscale Systems} \textbullet\
    \emph{Quantum Materials}.\\[2pt]
    Achieving quantum control requires:
    \textbf{(i)} preparing an initial state (RT1);
    \textbf{(ii)} steering its evolution (RT2);
    \textbf{(iii)} measuring the final state (RT3).
    RT4 provides the methodological backbone for all tracks.
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

